<%# ONE ACCOUNT, AND NOTHING INSIDE IT (two-ledger spec §2). See the UI design spec §4.3.
    Locals: `presenter` (HomePresenter), `account` (a Pool of type account).

    THE POOLS NESTED HERE ARE GONE (Task 6). An account used to hold its envelopes, so this section
    rendered `presenter.pools_for(account)` beneath the header; a category lives nowhere and holds
    its own money, so an account has exactly one thing to say — what the bank says — and the
    categories band above says the rest.

    The header carries `balance_of`, which for MAIN is the pot. It was `current_buffer_for`, and the
    word moved with the model: a "buffer" was an account's cash NET of the envelopes housed in it,
    and there is no such remainder now. The distribute screen calls AVAILABLE the buffer, so keeping
    the word here would have put one noun over two quantities on one screen.

    THE ` · target $2,000.00` CLAUSE IS DELETED (Task 7). It printed `pools.target_amount`, the
    "buffer marker", and the concept goes with the pool screens: two-ledger §2 gives accounts no
    target semantics — the buffer is AVAILABLE, on the other ledger — the last reader of the figure
    (`DistributionPresenter#buffer_target`) is deleted, the plan's T8 drops the column, and the
    account form that set it is now name-only. A number nothing can change and nothing reads is
    worse than no number.

    RENAME AND DELETE LIVE HERE NOW, because Home IS the accounts index: `pools/index` and
    `pools/show` carried those two buttons and both screens are deleted. %>
<% balance = presenter.balance_of(account) %>
<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" data-account-group="<%= account.name %>">
  <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-200 flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4">
    <%# Not the `uppercase` eyebrow this codebase gives short labels like "Current Balance": an
        account name is a proper noun the user typed, and rendering "Ally Savings" as ALLY SAVINGS
        shouts a name back at them. %>
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= account.name %></h3>
    <%# An overdraft is the loudest thing in the app and this is the row that owns the number, so
        it says so here too rather than leaving the standing band's sentence as the only place a
        negative balance is visible. %>
    <span class="text-sm <%= balance.negative? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-700" %>">
      balance now <strong><%= number_to_currency(balance) %></strong>
    </span>
  </div>

  <%# THE TWO DOORS AN ACCOUNT STILL HAS — and MAIN has only one of them. Deliberately quiet — this
      screen is about money, and a rename is housekeeping — but present, because deleting them with
      the Pools screens would have left a user no way to correct a typo in a bank name. %>
  <div class="px-6 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 flex justify-end items-baseline gap-3">
    <%# ** MAIN HAS NO DELETE BUTTON (final fix wave, C-1). ** `Pool#main_account_is_not_deletable`
        is the refusal and this is the affordance agreeing with it: main is on one side of every
        AccountMovement the app writes, so destroying it would take the whole physical ledger with
        it — the model halts, and a button that always ends in a red flash is worse than no button.

        THE LABEL RATHER THAN A DISABLED BUTTON, because the absence is a fact about the account
        rather than a state it might leave: main is main until the user changes it, and changing it
        is a future affordance this app does not have yet. The screen says which account this is,
        which is also the only place Home names the role at all. %>
    <% if presenter.main?(account) %>
      <span class="text-xs font-medium text-gray-500" data-account-main="<%= account.name %>">
        Main account — everything flows through it
      </span>
    <% end %>

    <%= link_to "Rename",
                edit_bank_account_path(account),
                class: "text-xs font-medium text-brand hover:text-brand-dark",
                data: { account_edit: account.name } %>

    <% unless presenter.main?(account) %>
      <%# ** THE CONFIRM SAYS WHAT DELETING AN ACCOUNT ACTUALLY DOES (fix round 1, MED-1; narrowed by
          Task 8; re-verified in the final fix wave). ** Its first draft carried the ENVELOPE sentence
          off the deleted `PoolsHelper#pool_delete_confirmation` — "its movements and any categories
          pointing at it move to your main account" — and every clause of it was false of an account.

          ONE FACT NOW, WHERE THERE WERE TWO, because the second stopped being true when the pool
          layer went: no category points at an account any more (`categories.pool_id` is dropped), so
          the "the delete is refused instead" clause described a refusal nothing can raise. What is
          left is the whole of what happens: `dependent: :destroy` on `movements_in`/`movements_out`
          deletes the account's transfers.

          "GOES BACK TO YOUR MAIN ACCOUNT" IS TRUE, CHECKED AGAINST THE WRITERS RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
          Both of them — `Entry#route_income_to!` and `AccountFundingsController#build_movement` —
          put `user.default_account` on `from_pool`, so main is on the OTHER side of every movement
          this account has. Deleting them subtracts nothing from main and restores each `moves out`
          it was carrying: the pot rises by exactly what this account held, and `pot + Σ accounts` is
          unchanged to the penny. The sentence is only ever rendered here, on a NON-main account,
          which is precisely the population it is true of. %>
      <% confirm = "Delete #{account.name}? The movements into and out of it are deleted with it, " \
                   "so the money they moved goes back to your main account and your balances will " \
                   "change. This action cannot be undone." %>
      <%= button_to "Delete",
                    bank_account_path(account),
                    method: :delete,
                    class: "text-xs font-medium text-status-danger hover:opacity-80",
                    form: { data: { turbo_confirm: confirm } },
                    data: { account_delete: account.name } %>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# ONBOARDING STEP 2 (main-account spec §5): `HomePresenter#awaiting_funding?` is the ONE gate,
      asked here and by AccountFundingsController. It reads `AccountLedger#balance_of` now — money
      is the only signal, and there is no envelope inside an account whose existence could ever
      have stood in for one. %>
  <% if presenter.awaiting_funding?(account) %>
    <%= render "fund_account",
        account: account,
        movement: presenter.funding_movement_for(account),
        main_account: presenter.user.default_account %>
  <% end %>

  <%# ONBOARDING STEP 3 (main-account spec §5): renders under MAIN alone, and only while the
      one-time latch is open — `HomePresenter#awaiting_opening_balance?` is the one gate. %>
  <% if presenter.awaiting_opening_balance?(account) %>
    <%= render "opening_balance", account: account %>
  <% end %>
</section>
